#import "/lib.typ": *
#show: docs-subchapter.with(
  ..dargs("Electrostatic Induction"),
)

It may appear that the condition set by a perfect conductor is rather forced and contrived. It could also be not entirely clear when such a situation might naturally arise. For this we point to the general phenomenon known as _polarization_, or _electrostatic induction_ in consideration of conductors; a process wherein charges within a conductor naturally rearrange themselves in presence of an external electric field $va(E)_"ext"$ to satisfy the conducting criteria.

This is made possible by the electric field $va(E)_"self"$ generated by the charges in a conductor itself, which by virtue of their especial configuration, opposes the external field $va(E)_"self" = -va(E)_"ext"$ within $V$. In a macroscopically neutral charge distribution, it is the Lorentz-averaged opposing charges that can be understood to separate to create such a deficit.

If it is understood that the passage of an electric field creates a net positive at one side of the conductor and negative at the other, then at long distances the effective electric field is characterized by a dipole field with
$
  va(p) = integral.surf_S va(r) sigma(va(r)) dif S.
$
It is imperative to understand that this model of charge separation and rearrangement is rather colloquial, and never does electrostatic induction in an actual metal involve large displacements of charges.

All physical systems rearrange their charge distribution $rho$ in order to minimize their total energy $U_"tot"$, which we may assume comprises the electrostatic energy $U_"E"$ and the energy due to additional quantum mechanical processes. A perfect conductor introduces the implicit assumption that the latter contributes only negligibly to $U_"tot"$, except for their effect in defining and fixing the shape of the body. Therefore under these precursory conditions, the minimization of $U_"tot"$ is equivalent to the minimization of $U_"E"$. In consideration of the latter we have
#theorem[Thomson's Theorem][
  The total electrostatic energy $U_"E"$ of a perfectly conducting body (in the presence of a fixed external electric field $-grad phi_"ext"$) of fixed shape and total charge $Q$ is minimized when $Q$ is distributed throughout $V$ in a way rendering $phi$ constant.
]
#proof[
  #let fncdv = $fdv(, rho(va(r)))$
  For simplicity we shall temporarily denote $va(xi)'' = va(r)'' - va(r)'$ and $va(xi)' = va(r)'' - va(r)$.

  The quantity wherein we are interested to minimize is precisely @eq:total-electrostatic-in-external-field, or
  $
    U_"E" [rho; phi_"ext"] = 1 / (8 uppi epsilon_0) integral.triple_V integral.triple_V (rho(va(r)'') rho(va(r)')) / xi'' dif^3 r' dif^3 r'' + integral.triple_V rho(va(r)') phi_"ext" (va(r)') dif^3 r',
  $
  under the functional constraint
  $
    integral.triple_V rho(va(r)') dif^3 r' = Q.
  $
  By the method of Lagrange multipliers in functional form, this is equivalent to the unconstrained (in $rho$ and $lambda$) optimization of
  $
    I[rho] = U_"E" [rho; phi_"ext"] - lambda (integral.triple_V rho(va(r)) dif^3 r - Q),
  $
  requiring $(delta I) / (delta rho(va(r))) = 0$. This then gives
  $
    (delta I) / (delta rho(va(r))) &= fncdv 1 / (8 uppi epsilon_0) integral.triple_V integral.triple_V (rho(va(r)'') rho(va(r)')) / xi'' dif^3 r' dif^3 r'' \
    &quad""+ fncdv integral.triple_V rho(va(r)') phi_"ext" (va(r)') dif^3 r' - lambda fncdv integral.triple_V rho(va(r)') dif^3 r' \
    &= 1 / (8 uppi epsilon_0) integral.triple_V integral.triple_V fncdv (rho(va(r)'') rho(va(r)')) / xi'' dif^3 r' dif^3 r'' \
    &quad""+ integral.triple_V fncdv [rho(va(r)') phi_"ext" (va(r)')] dif^3 r' - lambda integral.triple_V fncdv rho(va(r)') dif^3 r' \
    &= 1 / (8 uppi epsilon_0) integral.triple integral.triple [(rho(va(r)')) / xi'' delta(va(xi)') + (rho(va(r)'')) / xi'' delta(va(xi)) + rho(va(r)')rho(va(r)'') fdv(hfrc(1 / xi''), rho(va(r)))] dif^3 r' dif^3 r'' \
    &quad""+ integral.triple_V [delta(va(xi)) phi_"ext" (va(r)') + rho(va(r)') fncdv phi_"ext" (va(r)')] dif^3 r' - lambda integral.triple_V delta(va(xi)) dif^3 r' \
    &= 1 / (8 uppi epsilon_0) integral.triple integral.triple [(rho(va(r)')) / xi'' delta(va(xi)') + (rho(va(r)'')) / xi'' delta(va(xi)) + rho(va(r)')rho(va(r)'') fdv(hfrc(1 / xi''), rho(va(r)))] dif^3 r' dif^3 r'' \
    &quad""+ phi_"ext" (va(r)) - lambda \
    &= 1 / (8 uppi epsilon_0) [integral.triple (rho(va(r)')) / xi dif^3 r' + integral.triple (rho(va(r)'')) / xi' dif^3 r''] + phi_"ext" (va(r)) - lambda \
    &= phi_"self" (va(r)) + phi_"ext" (va(r)) - lambda = 0,
  $
  implying
  $
    phi_"tot" = phi_"self" + phi_"ext" = lambda ==> va(E)_"tot" = va(E)_"self" + va(E)_"ext" = 0.
  $
  It is also possible to ascertain the minimality thereof, by applying arbitrarily the virtual variation $rho -> rho + delta rho$ under the constraint $integral rho = integral (rho + delta rho) = Q$, whereby
  $
    U_"E" [rho + delta rho; phi_"ext"] - U_"E" [rho; phi_"ext"] &= U_"E" [rho] + U_"E" [delta rho] + V_"E" [rho; delta rho] + V_"E" [rho; phi_"ext"] + V_"E" [delta rho; phi_"ext"] \
    &quad""- U_"E" [rho] - V_"E" [rho; phi_"ext"] \
    &= U_"E" [delta rho] + V_"E" [rho; delta rho] + V_"E" [delta rho; phi_"ext"] \
    &= U_"E" [delta rho] + V_"E" [delta rho; phi_"self"] + V_"E" [delta rho; phi_"ext"] \
    &= U_"E" [delta rho] + V_"E" [delta rho; lambda #[(external potential)]] \
    &= U_"E" [delta rho] + lambda integral.triple delta rho dif^3 r \
    &= U_"E" [delta rho].
  $
  Since $U_"E" [delta rho]$ is positive definite quantity,
  $
    U_"E" [rho + delta rho; phi_"ext"] >= U_"E" [rho; phi_"ext"]. qedhere
  $
]
